        <field>AAO_Max_Findings_Per_Pass__c</field>
        <value xsi:type="xsd:double">60</value>
    </values>
    <!--
      EXTRACT-BIND-VERIFY, Charters v2.4 §P7.3. Three stages, three models.

      Stage 1 keeps claude-opus-5 and the high effort: it is the one call that reads the whole
      artifact and the only stage where inference is the work.

      Stage 2 is claude-sonnet-5. Separate from stage 3 by ruling, and separate in code:
      AAO_Extract.requireSeparateModels throws if these two strings ever match.

      Stage 3 is claude-haiku-4-5-20251001, which is the SMALLEST MODEL THIS CREDENTIAL
      REACHES, established by probe from Apex in this sandbox on 3 August rather than assumed:
      claude-3-5-haiku-20241022 returns 404 not_found_error through the same Named Credential.

      GROUP SIZES ARE MEASURED, ruling 6. Probed from Apex, 3 August, twelve realistic items
      per call, cold:
        stage 2  claude-sonnet-5            12 items  13,610 ms  1,252 in / 1,188 out
        stage 3  claude-haiku-4-5-20251001  12 items   6,444 ms    927 in /   428 out
        stage 3  claude-haiku-4-5-20251001   2 items   4,983 ms    402 in /   136 out
      The haiku pair gives a slope of ~146 ms per item on ~4,690 ms of fixed overhead, which
      is generation-bound rather than item-bound. Sonnet is ~1,140 ms per item including 562
      thinking tokens across the group.

      Both sizes are set so the PREDICTED elapsed is under half the 120-second cumulative
      per-transaction ceiling, because the variance that produced three timeouts on 3 August
      is the reason a comfortable margin is not overcaution:
        bind   24 items  predicted ~29 s
        verify 60 items  predicted ~13 s
      The run's per-stage elapsed either confirms these or falsifies them, and they move on
      the measurement and not on the estimate.
    -->
    <values>
        <field>AAO_Inventory_Model__c</field>
        <value xsi:type="xsd:string">claude-opus-5</value>
    </values>
    <values>
        <!--
          MEASURED, and measured the hard way. The first B&V run on this shape timed out at
          exactly 120.4 seconds with the shared 16,000-token bound: the callout went out 0.2 s
          into the transaction and the platform killed it at the ceiling. At the ~84 output
          tokens per second the strong model produces through this credential, 16,000 tokens is
          ~190 s of generation and could never have fitted. 6,000 is ~71 s of generation plus
          request latency, which leaves real margin against the variance that produced three
          timeouts on 3 August. If the inventory truncates at 6,000 the call throws on
          stop_reason=max_tokens and that is a finding to report, not a number to raise quietly.

          MEASURED AGAIN AT 6,000, and it truncated: 78,567 ms, 18,696 in / 6,000 out,
          cacheCreate 3,431, stop_reason=max_tokens. So the read FITS IN TIME and did not fit in
          the bound. 6,000 tokens in 78.4 s of callout is ~76.5 output tokens per second, which
          puts the absolute ceiling at ~9,180 tokens with no margin whatever.

          Raised to 7,500 — about 98 s of generation, ~20 s of margin — ONCE, together with the
          fix that put the ruled ~90-token evidence budget into the stage 1 prompt, where it had
          been missing. This is the last raise available: 9,180 is the arithmetic wall and
          nothing above ~8,000 leaves margin for the latency variance that produced three
          timeouts on 3 August. If 7,500 truncates too, the finding is that extract-once on an
          18,700-token artifact does not fit one bounded Apex callout, and that is design's to
          rule on, not mine to dial around.
        -->
        <field>AAO_Inventory_Max_Output_Tokens__c</field>
        <value xsi:type="xsd:double">7500</value>
    </values>
